 ## Background
-The concept of upsampling in volumetric data is rooted in the need to mitigate aliasing and improve the perceptual quality of synthesized views [S4]. Early work in colour volumetric compression highlighted that discrete domain positioning errors lead to poor subjective quality if the representing volume is not fine enough, motivating double-resolution pre-processing [S4]. In the context of deep learning for volume representation, upsampling layers are standard components of autoencoder decoders to reconstruct high-resolution features from compressed latent spaces [S2, unofficial]. More recently, specific implementations for 3D image resizing and cropping have been developed to handle aspect ratio mismatches in datasets used for neural rendering [G] (see [3D Image Resize and Crop](2703-3d-image-resize-and-crop.md)). Cloud rendering techniques have also adopted upscaling as a performance optimization, relying on the inherent blur of volumetric clouds to mask interpolation artifacts [S1, unofficial].
+Upsampling in volume rendering has evolved from simple geometric replication to complex, edge-aware solvers and deep learning-based reconstruction. Early methods focused on basic interpolation or tiling for efficiency [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. The introduction of the Fast Bilateral Solver adapted 2D edge-preserving smoothing to 3D volumes, allowing for high-fidelity upscaling of similarity maps guided by intensity data [S1, unofficial]. More recently, deep learning approaches, such as the SRNO model, have been integrated into implicit neural representations to denoise and super-resolve medical volume data, addressing artifacts common in traditional methods [S5]. Pre-processing techniques like double-resolution mapping have also been developed to correct discrete positioning errors in view synthesis [S4].
 
